# Preserve Git History on File Split

When **one tracked file is split into two**, the successor that inherits **most of the original code** must keep git history via `git mv`. The smaller extracted portion is a **new file**.

## Required workflow

1. Compare the two resulting files — identify which will contain more of the original code.
2. `git mv old/path larger_successor/path` (rename allowed).
3. Edit the renamed file in place: remove extracted code, update namespace/class name.
4. Create the smaller extract as a **new file** with only the extracted logic.

## Do not

- Delete the original and create two new files at different paths (loses `git log --follow`).
- Use plain `mv` / editor move without `git mv`.

## 1 → many (original path must remain)

When the original path must stay (e.g. `Program.cs` as entry point), keep history on the original file. All extracted modules are new files.

## Example

```bash
# WeatherDataCsvWriter.cs (~117 lines) → parsed writer (~70) + normalized writer (~40)
git mv Processor/WeatherDataCsvWriter.cs \
       Processor/Parsing/ParsedWeatherDataCsvWriter.cs
# Edit ParsedWeatherDataCsvWriter.cs; create NormalizedWeatherDataCsvWriter.cs fresh
```

After the split, `git status` should show `renamed:` for the history-carrying file.
